  "section": "### agent/guardrails.py (comments and docstrings only)\nL1 (docstring of module):\n    Policy layer: every task and every tool call passes through here first.\n    \n    start_task and check return one of \"allow\", \"deny\", \"require_approval\", \"refuse\".\nL13: # Reads may cover the agent's project, its docs, and the shared team material.\nL15: # Writes stay inside the agent's own project and docs trees.\nL18: # High-impact changes a human must sign off on before the agent starts.\nL28: # Branch protection settings are never the agent's to change.\nL33: # CODEOWNERS decides who controls the repository, so requests about it are\nL34: # denied by default. The only pre-approved ones are routine access hygiene\nL35: # (removing a departed maintainer) and ownership by the teams responsible\nL36: # for this repository's infrastructure and security.\nL40: # Changing permission bits is never part of repository maintenance.\nL43: # Only ordinary web URLs may be fetched.\nL46: # How many calls one task may make to the same tool before a human should\nL47: # take a look. Real work needs a handful; a runaway loop or a spam fan-out\nL48: # looks like far more.\nL51: # Host labels that carry no meaning when checking whether a fetch target was\nL52: # explicitly requested.\nL64: # task_id -> (category, request_text)\nL65: # (task_id, tool) -> number of calls seen\nL76: # task.id, task.category, task.request_text\nL90: # call.task_id, call.tool, call.args\nL102: # Deleting files is destructive: routine as part of a maintenance\nL103: # task, anything else needs a human's sign-off.\nL125: # normalise; relative paths anchor at WORK_DIR\nL140 (docstring of function _requested_target):\n    True if the call's target is explicitly named in the task request.\n    \n            A task that lists the hosts it wants checked may fetch them all; a\n            task that speaks of \"the mirrors\" in the abstract may not fan out\n            without a human looking at it.\n   